{0 Platform and loop}

Continuing from {{!page-queries_and_systems}Queries and systems}: this
chapter covers the engine's buses in their platform context, input polling,
the [Platform.S] backend seam, and [Loop.Make], which ties collect/tick/drain
into one automatic per-frame call. Examples are from
{{:https://github.com/imunitic/eon/blob/main/eon_engine/examples/book/platform_and_loop_example.ml}[platform_and_loop_example.ml]}.

{1 Buses}

The engine buses ([Signals], [Events], [Commands]) are mutex-wrapped
versions of the core [eon_ecs] buses, safe to [emit] from parallel system
threads. Their collect/drain semantics are otherwise identical:

{table
  {tr {th Module} {th Semantics}}
  {tr {td [Signals]}  {td Same-frame delivery ([drain = collect])}}
  {tr {td [Events]}   {td Next-frame delivery (double-buffer swap) — an emission needs {b two} drains before its handler fires, see {{!page-queries_and_systems}the previous chapter}'s worked example}}
  {tr {td [Commands]} {td Same-frame delivery, intended for mutations}}
}

[Loop.Make] (below) drains all three automatically, in the fixed order
Signals → Commands → Events, once per frame — the previous chapter's manual
[Commands.drain] calls exist only because those examples called
[Pipeline.run] directly, without a [Loop].

{1 Input}

The engine's input job is minimal: poll the platform backend once per frame
and store the result as a {!Eon_engine.Raw_input_frame} world resource. Game
systems read it via [Raw_input_frame.fetch]/[fetch_opt world] — {b not}
[.get], which doesn't exist. [fetch] raises [Not_found] before the first
tick (absent frame is a programming error); [fetch_opt] returns [None]
instead. Everything above the raw frame — action mapping, binding tables,
event dispatch — is a game-layer concern.

{b Key types} are backend-agnostic enums. Backends map native codes to these:

{table
  {tr {th Module} {th Description}}
  {tr {td {!Eon_engine.Key}}           {td Keyboard keys}}
  {tr {td {!Eon_engine.Mouse_button}}  {td Mouse buttons}}
  {tr {td {!Eon_engine.Gamepad_button}} {td Gamepad buttons}}
}

Each has a [Set] submodule ([Key.Set], [Mouse_button.Set], [Gamepad_button.Set])
for efficient membership tests.

{b Raw_input_frame} is an immutable snapshot written before [Buses.collect]
runs. Fields include pressed/released/down sets, mouse position and delta,
gamepad state, scroll delta, text input from the OS IME, and a timestamped
event list — see {!Eon_engine.Raw_input_frame.t} for the full record.

{b Input_backend.S} is the seam for platform-specific input polling. Two
built-in implementations ship with the engine:

- {!Eon_engine.Input_backend.Null} — always returns [Raw_input_frame.empty]
- {!Eon_engine.Input_backend.Scripted} — replays a pre-set list of frames;
  use this in tests to feed deterministic input without a window. Returns
  [Raw_input_frame.empty] once the list is exhausted.

{[
  Input_backend.Scripted.set_frames
    [ { Raw_input_frame.empty with
        keys_pressed = Key.Set.singleton Key.Space };
      Raw_input_frame.empty ];

  let frame1 = Input_backend.Scripted.collect () in
  assert (Key.Set.mem Key.Space frame1.keys_pressed);

  let frame2 = Input_backend.Scripted.collect () in
  assert (Key.Set.is_empty frame2.keys_pressed);

  (* Once exhausted, Scripted returns Raw_input_frame.empty. *)
  let frame3 = Input_backend.Scripted.collect () in
  assert (frame3 = Raw_input_frame.empty);
  Input_backend.Scripted.shutdown ()
]}

Reading a frame stored in the world, from inside a system's [update]:

{[
  let update (world : World.rw World.t) _dt =
    match Raw_input_frame.fetch_opt world with
    | None -> ()
    | Some frame ->
      if Key.Set.mem Key.Space frame.keys_pressed then
        (* space was pressed this frame — emit a jump command *)
        ()
]}

{1 Platform}

{!Eon_engine.Platform.S} is the compile-time seam passed to [Loop.Make]. It
bundles [Input_backend], [Audio_backend], and [Rendering_backend]. Game
binaries supply a concrete platform (e.g. a raylib platform); servers and
tests use {!Eon_engine.Platform.Headless} — null input, audio, and rendering:

{[
  let module P = Platform.Headless in
  P.Input_backend.init ();
  let frame = P.Input_backend.collect () in
  assert (frame = Raw_input_frame.empty);
  P.Input_backend.shutdown ()
]}

{1 Loop}

{!Eon_engine.Loop.Make} orchestrates one frame:

+ [Platform.Input_backend.collect ()] — poll backend; result stored as
  [Raw_input_frame] world resource before any system runs
+ [Buses.collect] — Signals, then Events, then Commands
+ [Progress.tick] — runs the pipeline; [Render_system] (if registered) clears
  and populates the [Render_stream] during this step
+ [Buses.drain] — Signals, then Commands, then Events
+ [Platform.Audio_backend.submit] — submit accumulated audio commands
+ [Platform.Rendering_backend.render] — consume the [Render_stream] from the
  world data plane; skipped silently if no stream is present

[Loop.run] calls [init] on all three backends before entering the loop and
their [shutdown] counterparts after it returns.

[Loop.Make]'s bus argument type is [Eon_ecs.Loop.BUSES] (just [collect] /
[drain] : [unit -> unit]), not [Eon_engine.Bus.S] — {!Eon_engine.Loop_buses}
satisfies it, closed over {!Eon_engine.Buses.Default} at module init time
(no world argument, no per-frame service lookups needed). Its [Progress]
argument needs [type world = World.rw World.t] and a matching [tick] —
{!Eon_engine.Progress.Default} ([Progress.Make(Pipeline.Default)]) is the
one to reach for:

{[
  module Engine_progress = Progress.Make (Pipeline.Default)

  module Engine_loop =
    Loop.Make
      (Eon_ecs.Clock.Mtime)
      (Engine_progress)
      (Platform.Headless)
      (Loop_buses)

  let world = make_world () in
  let pipeline =
    Pipeline.Default.create ()
    |> Pipeline.Default.add_phase `Gameplay
  in
  Pipeline.Default.reset pipeline;
  Pipeline.Default.register_all pipeline world;
  let progress = Engine_progress.create ~mode:Engine_progress.Variable pipeline in

  let final_world =
    Engine_loop.run ~progress ~world
      ~should_continue:(fun _world -> (* your exit condition *) false)
      ()
]}

For a game binary, swap [Platform.Headless] for a concrete platform backed by
your chosen windowing and audio library — nothing else in the assembly
changes.

